    ('3bb2d9e1-7e77-4582-8b28-92ab27133a9a'::uuid, 'cultural_influence', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-12b-rollback:3bb2d9e1-7e77-4582-8b28-92ab27133a9a:cultural_influence', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-12b: cultural_influence ''8'' restored on Wandlebury Hill', '0bcfa964f52ef4f8f127543efade0a23', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "cultural_influence = 7 from description yes, thumbnail no, 3 content link(s), 2 image(s), source_url yes"}, {"source": "production export 07ceaa5910522703 (2026-11-12 12:30:18.70478+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 0 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59472 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-12''s own export (2026-11-12 10:36:17.26111+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33675", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0024:chunk-0001: description ''Wandlebury Hill is a 74-metre chalk peak in the Gog Magog Hills, southeast of Cambridge, England[1]. Bronze Age people inhabited the site, and around 500 BC an Iron Age hill fort called Wandlebury Ring was constructed here[2]. At 74 metres, Wandlebury Hill is the same height as nearby Little Trees Hill, though the latter is a more notable landmark[1].'' -> ''Wandlebury Hill is a peak in the Gog Magog Hills, a ridge of low chalk hills extending for several miles to the southeast of Cambridge [1]. Wandlebury was already inhabited in the Bronze Age and 2500 years ago there was an Iron Age hill fort here known as Wandlebury Ring [1]. This hill fort once had concentric ditches and earthen walls which were kept in place by wooden palisades [1]. Although the fort has vanished, the ditch (the Ring) dug around the edge can clearly be seen and walked along [1]. There is no evidence that it was ever used in defence [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:116214", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "202