.org/wiki/Gro%C3%9Fmugl'' -> ''https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leeberg_(Gro%C3%9Fmugl)''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153095", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-016: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/774a99e0/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/774a99e0/Großmugl_-_Hügelgrab_(1).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:338413", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4324:chunk-0001: description ''The Leeberg at Grossmugl in Lower Austria is the largest burial mound of the entire Hallstatt culture, standing approximately 14-15m high with a 55m diameter. Erected c. 600-500 BC by the Kalenderberg culture, a northeastern subgroup of the Hallstatt culture, it likely served as the burial place of a powerful ruler. The tumulus has never been excavated and this will not change in the foreseeable future. A smaller adjacent mound, the \"Tomb of the Queen,\" investigated in the 1950s, yielded clay vessels with bull-head decorations and a rider-and-horse sculpture. Modern non-destructive surveys have documented an extensive Iron Age cult site and settlement landscape around the mound.'' -> ''The tumulus has never been excavated and this will not change in the foreseeable future [1] [2]. A smaller adjacent mound, the \"Tomb of the Queen,\" investigated in the 1950s, yielded clay vessels with bull-head decorations and a rider-and-horse sculpture [3] [4]. Modern non-destructive surveys have documented an extensive Iron Age cult site and settlement landscape around the mound [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
